          ":question/id": "4360bcb1-959b-53b1-855f-91f5d582be11",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-107123",
          ":question/problem": "A pork leg is taken out of the oven with a temperature of ${150}^{\u2218}C,$ and placed in a room that maintains a temperature of ${24}^{\u2218}C$, and its temperature begins to decrease. After $15$ minutes, the  temperature of the pork leg is ${100}^{\u2218}C.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the pork leg to have a temperature of ${60}^{\u2218}C?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the pork leg decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the pork leg was transferred into the ${24}^{\u2218}C$ room.*",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={24}^{\u2218}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-24).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-24} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-24} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061|T-24| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-24>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-24 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-24 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-24 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =24+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the pork leg was ${150}^{\u2218}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=150,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}150 & =24+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 150 & =24+K \\\\ K & =126\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=24+126{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $15$ minutes, the temperature of the pork leg is ${100}^{\u2218}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(15)=100,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & =24+126{e}^{-15k} \\\\ 76 & =126{e}^{-15k} \\\\ \\frac{38}{63} & ={e}^{-15k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061\\left(\\frac{38}{63}\\right) & =-15k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{15}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061\\left(\\frac{38}{63}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the pork leg has a temperature of ${60}^{\u2218}C.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =60 \\\\ 24+126{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =60 \\\\ 126{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =36 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{2}{7} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061\\left(\\frac{2}{7}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061\\left(\\frac{2}{7}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the pork leg will have a temperature of ${60}^{\u2218}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061\\left(\\frac{2}{7}\\right)}{-\\frac{1}{15}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061\\left(\\frac{38}{63}\\right)}\u224837\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute."
        },
