          ":question/problem": "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a body can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.06(T-21)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the body is ${40}^{\u2218}C,$ calculate the temperature of the body after $4$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "We're told that the temperature of the body is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.06(T-21).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-21} & =-0.06\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-21} & =-\\int 0.06\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061|T-21| & =-0.06t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-21>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-21 & ={e}^{-0.06t+C} \\\\ T-21 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.06t} \\\\ T-21 & =K{e}^{-0.06t} \\\\ T & =21+K{e}^{-0.06t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the body was ${40}^{\u2218}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=40,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40 & =21+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 40 & =21+K \\\\ K & =19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=21+19{e}^{-0.06t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the body after $t=4$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(4)=21+19{e}^{-0.06\\cdot 4}\u2248{36}^{\u2218}\\text{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."
        }
      ],
