              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-541",
              ":question/problem": "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\cos(3y)\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + \\sin(2x) = 0$ given that  $y\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = 0.$ Assume that $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} < y < \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} .$",
              ":question/worked-solution": "We first find the general solution, and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x) \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int \\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3y)\\,\\text{d}y & =-\\int \\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(3y) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}\u2061(3y) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x)+{C}_{1}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C_1=3C.$\n\nNow let's apply the condition $y\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = 0$ to find $C_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}\u2061(3\\cdot 0) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}\u2061(0) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+{C}_{1} \\\\ 0 & =0+{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the particular solution in implicit form:\n\n$$\n\\sin(3y)   =  \\dfrac {3} {2} \\cos(2x)\n$$"
            },
